  "profile": {
    "video_id": "kenji:a_KAzqQAtFw",
    "purpose": "A recipe demonstration of Cantonese-style steamed fish made with salmon fillets. It covers aromatics prep, pulling pin bones, steaming to a target core temperature with a predictive thermometer, a quick soy-based sauce, and a sizzling hot-oil finish.",
    "video_type": "how_to",
    "thesis": "Steaming fish Cantonese style, topped with cold-soaked aromatics and finished with smoking-hot oil, is one of the easiest and most delicious ways to cook fish. A predictive thermometer helps nail the doneness.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "The dish is traditionally made with a whole fish such as flounder or branzino; here it uses center-cut king salmon fillets.",
      "Ginger trimmings and scallion greens go under the fish on the plate to keep it elevated while it steams.",
      "Garlic, julienned ginger, lengthwise-sliced scallions and cilantro are soaked in cold water in a salad spinner. This mellows the pungent aromatics and makes the herbs plump and curl into a crunchy salad.",
      "Salmon pin bones are pulled before cooking: rub against the grain to pop them up, then pull in the direction they face with pliers or fish tweezers. Firmer fish like black cod or escolar can be deboned after cooking.",
      "The salmon steams in a bamboo steamer over a wok until the core reaches about 105°, roughly 7 minutes, for a rare-to-medium-rare center.",
      "The sauce is about a teaspoon of sugar, a couple of tablespoons of light soy sauce, a tablespoon of rice wine and a tablespoon of water. He uses sake; Shaoxing wine or dry sherry are the usual choices.",
      "After steaming, the flesh is slid off the skin. It is plated with sauce, half the aromatic salad and sliced chili, then smoking-hot neutral oil is poured over, and the rest of the salad goes on top.",
      "Aromatic oil from his cookbook club recipe can replace the neutral oil for more flavor."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
        "title": "Intro and aromatics prep",
        "summary": "Kenji introduces Cantonese-style steamed salmon. He slices garlic, trims ginger and scallions (trimmings go on the plate as a rack), cuts the scallions lengthwise, and soaks the aromatics in cold water.",
        "first_segment": 0,
        "last_segment": 2
      },
      {
        "title": "Cilantro, ginger julienne and removing salmon pin bones",
        "summary": "He adds cilantro and julienned ginger to the soak. He then shows how to find and pull pin bones from king salmon and explains why to do it before cooking for delicate fish but after cooking for fish like black cod.",
        "first_segment": 3,
        "last_segment": 5
      },
      {
        "title": "Steaming with the thermometer and making the sauce",
        "summary": "The skin-on salmon goes onto the aromatics with a predictive thermometer inserted and steams in a bamboo steamer over a wok to about 105°. Meanwhile he mixes a sugar, light soy, sake and water sauce and slices a Fresno chili.",
        "first_segment": 6,
        "last_segment": 8
      },
      {
        "title": "Plating and the hot-oil finish",
        "summary": "He drains and spins the aromatics and separates the salmon from its skin. He plates it with sauce, salad and chili, then pours smoking oil over it, explaining what the cold-water soak does.",
        "first_segment": 8,
        "last_segment": 10
      },
      {
        "title": "Tasting and wrap-up",
        "summary": "Kenji tastes the salmon and suggests using aromatic oil from his cookbook club. He promotes the thermometer and closes with a channel/Patreon outro.",
        "first_segment": 11,
        "last_segment": 12
      }
    ],
